WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.654
- There's this really pivotal scene

00:00:01.654 --> 00:00:04.383
in the Thanksgiving episode
of the show "Masters of None".

00:00:04.383 --> 00:00:06.033
Angela Bassett plays a mom.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.302
She's a mother to Denise.

00:00:07.302 --> 00:00:10.232
She turns to her, she says,
"Honey, we're minorities

00:00:10.232 --> 00:00:14.400
"and we have to work twice
as hard to get half as far."

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.150
(slow acoustic guitar music)

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.296
For me, Imposter Syndrome,
there was never one big moment.

00:00:35.296 --> 00:00:36.800
It was all kind of like smaller moments.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.166
One of the first ones I felt was

00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:40.712
I went to a very diverse high school.

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:42.728
There was like Latinos, black people,

00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:45.632
Asian people, Indian
people, and I ended up going

00:00:45.632 --> 00:00:48.398
to college in Providence, Rhode Island,

00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:50.176
and I went to this liberal arts college.

00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:51.575
I was there on a scholarship,

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:53.768
and I remember my first day on campus.

00:00:53.768 --> 00:00:55.246
It was a sea of white people,

00:00:55.246 --> 00:00:59.095
and I was so surprised, and
I just thought the world

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:00.542
was like my high school.

00:01:00.542 --> 00:01:03.790
I never felt such a lack of diversity.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.894
It was really shocking to
me, and I felt insecure.

00:01:06.894 --> 00:01:09.328
I think I worked hard because
I wanted to convince myself

00:01:09.328 --> 00:01:10.328
that I belonged there.

00:01:10.328 --> 00:01:14.495
- To me, Imposter Syndrome is
a person who never takes in

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.399
their successes and always
feels like they're waiting

00:01:19.399 --> 00:01:21.959
on the next shoe to drop,
or waiting on somebody

00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:23.198
to find them out.

00:01:23.198 --> 00:01:26.023
When I started my company Mae B,

00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:28.869
which is a stationary
company for women of color,

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:32.606
I remember hitting these
really big milestones,

00:01:32.606 --> 00:01:35.734
and one of them was I was
featured in Essence Magazine

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:38.567
six months into starting my business,

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:40.862
and I could never take that in.

00:01:40.862 --> 00:01:42.119
I could never say, you know what?

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:44.342
This happened because I was bold enough

00:01:44.342 --> 00:01:45.799
to reach out to the magazine.

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:48.470
This happened because I started
this company from scratch.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:49.950
It was never about what I did.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.414
It was about, well maybe
it was just the universe,

00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:54.949
but it wasn't about my
hard work, dedication

00:01:54.949 --> 00:01:55.990
to making that happen.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.560
- My first paid internship
was a non-profit in D.C.,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.917
and it was for an internship
program for my college,

00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.159
and other colleges in
California and also had

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:07.038
a couple of Ivy League
schools involved with it.

00:02:07.038 --> 00:02:10.439
I was one of maybe five students
that had a paid internship,

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:12.470
and there were about 200
students in the program.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.934
I kept telling myself
that I didn't deserve

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.054
to have a paid internship.

00:02:16.054 --> 00:02:18.213
The Ivy kids didn't
have a paid internship.

00:02:18.213 --> 00:02:22.503
It didn't make sense to me
despite having worked before

00:02:22.503 --> 00:02:25.471
and being a politics major and
being in D.C. where politics

00:02:25.471 --> 00:02:28.542
is such a big thing, I just
told myself that it was luck.

00:02:28.542 --> 00:02:31.824
Kids who I felt were more
qualified or deserved it

00:02:31.824 --> 00:02:34.416
more than I did, didn't have
the same experience that I had.

00:02:34.416 --> 00:02:37.150
- Once I started becoming a
manager and leading teams,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.719
and being the head of
departments and things like that,

00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:42.237
more often than not, not
only am I the only woman,

00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:44.022
but I'm the only person of color.

00:02:44.022 --> 00:02:47.550
- When you're a black woman,
you don't get to be mediocre.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.118
You don't get to walk
into a place and say,

00:02:49.118 --> 00:02:51.870
you know what, hmm, I'm just
gonna be all right today.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.317
- I think black women are taught

00:02:54.317 --> 00:02:57.933
that we are not deserving, as
much as I hate to say that,

00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:01.790
and I think that it stuck
with me unfortunately.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.542
I work hard and consciously I feel

00:03:04.542 --> 00:03:06.388
that I am deserving of things,

00:03:06.388 --> 00:03:09.107
but subconsciously I don't feel that way.

00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:11.085
I attribute everything to luck

00:03:11.085 --> 00:03:13.397
as opposed to just attributing it to me.

00:03:13.397 --> 00:03:16.093
- I think Imposters
Syndrome is something that

00:03:16.093 --> 00:03:18.900
as women of color, we're
always going to come across,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.524
and it's really how do you handle it?

00:03:20.524 --> 00:03:23.155
Feeling alone, feeling like a fraud,

00:03:23.155 --> 00:03:25.191
feeling like wait, how did I get here?

00:03:25.191 --> 00:03:26.703
Do I deserve to be here?

00:03:26.703 --> 00:03:28.542
- And there's pressure as a black women

00:03:28.542 --> 00:03:31.301
to always be strong,
to always be fearless,

00:03:31.301 --> 00:03:34.384
to always have this hardened exterior

00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.846
because if we show any part of ourselves

00:03:37.846 --> 00:03:40.918
that is actually human,
then that makes us weak,

00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:42.489
or that makes us less valuable.

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:44.251
- I have to keep telling
myself that everyone

00:03:44.251 --> 00:03:46.666
does not necessarily know more than me,

00:03:46.666 --> 00:03:49.058
or deserved to be here any more than I do.

00:03:49.058 --> 00:03:51.508
- These are things that
I have to remind myself

00:03:51.508 --> 00:03:54.314
every single day because if I don't,

00:03:54.314 --> 00:03:56.066
I'll fall back into this
place where it's like

00:03:56.066 --> 00:03:57.867
okay, what happens when
somebody doesn't think

00:03:57.867 --> 00:04:00.596
I'm good enough or am
I actually good enough?

00:04:00.596 --> 00:04:02.755
- And there's that old saying,
"fake it 'til you make it",

00:04:02.755 --> 00:04:05.091
and I just kind of try to do that

00:04:05.091 --> 00:04:06.891
until I realized I'm
not faking it anymore.

00:04:06.891 --> 00:04:09.395
I'm just like, I'm just slaying it.

00:04:09.395 --> 00:04:13.562
(slow, relaxing electronic music)

00:04:21.537 --> 00:04:22.553
(creaking sign)

